---
title: Audit log
name: Audit log
description: Persist redacted administrator write events and build a scoped, read-only viewer.
---

sveltekit-admin does not ship an `AuditLog` table or a dedicated log UI. It
provides an opt-in `audit` callback after every **successful** create, update,
or delete. The callback receives a redacted `AuditEvent`; persistence and
viewing are application concerns.

Reads (GET), logout, and `_search` are not audited. A POST that fails
validation or the database write does not call `audit`. If `audit` is omitted,
there are no extra reads or calls.

## A production-oriented Prisma model

This model keeps the event envelope queryable while storing snapshots in JSON.
`actorId` is a string so an application can use either numeric or string user
IDs; the callback below normalizes the value. Add a tenant key when the same
database contains more than one tenant.

```text
model AuditLog {
  id        String   @id @default(cuid())
  at        DateTime @default(now())
  actorId   String?
  actorName String?
  action    String
  model     String
  recordId  String
  values    Json?
  before    Json?
  after     Json?
  changes   Json?
  m2m       Json?
  tenantId  String?

  @@index([at])
  @@index([model, recordId])
  @@index([actorId, at])
  @@index([tenantId, at])
}
```

Use a compound index that matches the viewer's most common filter (for
example, `[tenantId, at]`). Keep the log append-oriented: avoid exposing the
JSON snapshots to ordinary application code, and decide whether the database
should enforce retention, partitioning, or archival before the table grows.

## Persist the callback

The exact callback type is `AuditEvent`. Its `action` discriminant determines
which fields exist:

| Field | Present on | Meaning |
| ----- | ---------- | ------- |
| `event` | every event | The SvelteKit `RequestEvent`; read the actor from `event.locals`. |
| `at` | every event | Timestamp taken after the write succeeds. |
| `action` | every event | `'create'`, `'update'`, or `'delete'`. |
| `model` | every event | Schema model name, such as `User`. |
| `id` | every event | Primary key; on create it is the adapter-returned generated ID. |
| `values` | create/update | Redacted submitted scalar payload. |
| `before` | update/delete | Redacted pre-write snapshot, or `null` if it could not be read. |
| `after` | create/update | Redacted post-write snapshot. |
| `changes` | update | `{ from, to }` only for fields that changed; `{}` when `before` is `null`. |
| `m2m` | create/update when submitted | Relation field to submitted IDs, including `[]` when cleared. |

Put the signed-in administrator in `event.locals` in your auth `handle` (see
[Authentication](/docs/authentication)). Then persist the event without
reconstructing or bypassing its redaction:

```typescript
import { createAdminHandler } from 'sveltekit-admin';
import { prisma } from '$lib/server/prisma';

// Prisma Json does not accept Date or bigint values directly.
const jsonValue = (value: unknown) =>
  value === undefined
    ? undefined
    : JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(value, (_key, item) =>
        typeof item === 'bigint' ? item.toString() : item
      ));

export const handle = createAdminHandler({
  prisma,
  authCheck: (event) => event.locals.session?.user?.role === 'admin',
  audit: async (entry) => {
    // Prevent recursion if AuditLog is left visible in the admin.
    if (entry.model === 'AuditLog') return;

    const actor = entry.event.locals.session?.user;
    const data = {
      at: entry.at,
      actorId: actor?.id == null ? null : String(actor.id),
      actorName: actor?.name ?? actor?.email ?? null,
      tenantId: actor?.tenantId == null ? null : String(actor.tenantId),
      action: entry.action,
      model: entry.model,
      recordId: String(entry.id),
      values: 'values' in entry ? jsonValue(entry.values) : undefined,
      before: 'before' in entry ? jsonValue(entry.before) : undefined,
      after: 'after' in entry ? jsonValue(entry.after) : undefined,
      changes: 'changes' in entry ? jsonValue(entry.changes) : undefined,
      m2m: 'm2m' in entry ? jsonValue(entry.m2m) : undefined
    };

    await prisma.auditLog.create({ data });
  }
});
```

The library strips fields whose names contain `password`, `hash`, `secret`, or
`token`, as well as fields listed in `models[].hidden`, from `values`,
`before`, `after`, and `changes`. Do not add raw request data or query the
original row again in the callback to “fill in” omitted fields: that defeats
the redaction boundary. Treat `event.locals` as your actor source and store
only the identity your authorization layer has already established.

The callback is awaited before the 303 redirect, so a successful
`prisma.auditLog.create` commits before the response. There is no transaction
between the adapter write and your sink. If the callback throws, the mutation
still redirects and the handler logs
`[sveltekit-admin] audit callback failed:`; monitor that error if audit records
are compliance-critical.

## A scoped, read-only viewer in the admin

Leave `AuditLog` out of `exclude` if you want it to appear as a normal admin
model. Mark every editable scalar as `readonly`, select only useful list
columns, and apply both authorization and tenant scoping:

```typescript
createAdminHandler({
  prisma,
  authCheck: (event) => {
    const user = event.locals.session?.user;
    return (user?.role === 'admin' || user?.role === 'auditor') && user?.tenantId != null;
  },
  models: {
    AuditLog: {
      listFields: ['at', 'actorName', 'action', 'model', 'recordId', 'tenantId'],
      readonly: [
        'at', 'actorId', 'actorName', 'action', 'model', 'recordId',
        'values', 'before', 'after', 'changes', 'm2m', 'tenantId'
      ],
      scope: (ctx) => ({
        tenantId: ctx.locals?.session?.user?.tenantId
      })
    }
  },
  audit: async (entry) => {
    if (entry.model === 'AuditLog') return;
    // Persist the redacted entry as shown above.
  }
});
```

The example intentionally admits only tenant-bound admins/auditors. A global
administrator needs a separate explicit policy (for example, a dedicated
cross-tenant viewer); do not silently treat a missing `tenantId` as “all
tenants.”

`models[].scope` is the authorization boundary for this model: it is composed
into list, count, detail, edit, delete, and mutation queries. It must return a
non-empty condition for every request; an empty object is rejected rather than
being treated as “no scope.” `models[].listWhere` is different: it scopes only
the list view (including its search, filters, count, and pagination). Do not
use `listWhere` alone to protect tenant data.

The example's `scope` assumes `tenantId` is present in `event.locals` for every
authorized request. Adapt the field and locals path to your schema. Never fall
back to an unscoped query when the tenant is missing. If logs are global,
replace the tenant scope with an explicit policy rather than accidentally
making them visible to every authenticated user.

`readonly` prevents field editing, but it does not make a model an immutable,
append-only store or remove the model's delete action. If administrators must
never delete audit records, use `exclude: ['AuditLog']` and provide a separate
viewer with a deny-delete policy, or enforce retention and delete permissions
outside this package. Excluding the model also means its writes cannot recurse
through the admin UI; keep the callback guard when other code can write it.

## Retention, indexing, and security checklist

- [ ] Persist only the redacted event fields; never log raw `FormData`, request
      headers, cookies, tokens, passwords, or a second unredacted ORM snapshot.
- [ ] Resolve the actor from the authenticated `event.locals`, and store a
      stable ID plus a display label only if your privacy policy permits it.
- [ ] Require an explicit tenant scope for tenant-owned logs. Test missing,
      expired, and cross-tenant sessions; fail closed instead of returning `{}`.
- [ ] Restrict the viewer with `authCheck`, mark columns `readonly`, and avoid
      putting large JSON snapshots in `listFields` if the list becomes costly.
- [ ] Decide whether audit records themselves are sensitive: protect JSON
      snapshots, database backups, exports, and support tooling accordingly.
- [ ] Index the time range and the filters the viewer uses (`tenantId`,
      `model`, `recordId`, or `actorId`); do not index every JSON property by
      default.
- [ ] Define retention, archival, and deletion controls before production.
      Database-level partitioning or an external immutable sink may be more
      appropriate for compliance workloads.
- [ ] Monitor callback failures. The business write remains successful when the
      callback throws, so a failed sidecar must be observable and recoverable.
